<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy General of Venezuela</h1><p class="st-meta">1777 CE – 1823 CE · capital Caracas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish colonial unit that first united the provinces of Venezuela under Caracas and became the starting point of Bolívar's revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 8 September 1777 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III</a> united the provinces of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumana">Cumaná</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayana-province">Guayana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarita-island">Margarita</a> under the captain-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, separating them from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a> for military and financial purposes. It built on the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-venezuela">Province of Venezuela</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>An intendancy for finance was created in 1776, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in 1786 and a merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> in 1793, giving Caracas real autonomy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">Cacao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> were exported by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> planter elite using enslaved labour. Revolutionary ideas spread, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> led a failed invasion in 1806. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> in 1797.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 19 April 1810 a junta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> deposed the captain-general, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-republic-of-venezuela">First Republic of Venezuela</a> declared independence on 5 July 1811. Years of war followed until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carabobo">Carabobo</a> in 1821; the last royalists left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a> in 1823.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-general-of-venezuela.jpg" alt="Captaincy General of Venezuela" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Salvadoroff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitan%C3%ADa_General_de_Venezuela_proyecci%C3%B3n_ortogr%C3%A1fica.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1777 CE – 1823 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caracas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Guevara Vasconcelos, Vicente Emparan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Venezuela, Guyana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Capitanía General de Venezuela</dd></dl></aside></div>
